const faq = [
  { q: 'What was the Viking raven banner?', a: 'The raven banner (Old Norse: hrafnsmerki, also called Hrafn, "raven," or Landeyda, "land-devastator") was a war standard used by Viking Age Scandinavian chieftains and kings in battle. It depicted a raven — the bird of Odin — and was believed by those who carried it to bring victory in battle, provided it was sewn and consecrated correctly. The banner is recorded in several Norse sagas, Anglo-Saxon chronicles, and in Irish annals.' },
  { q: 'Did the raven banner really exist?', a: 'Yes. The raven banner is attested in multiple independent historical sources as a real military standard used in the Viking Age (c. 793–1066 CE). The Annals of St Neots (c. 10th century) record a raven banner captured from a Danish army at the Battle of Ashdown (871 CE). The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle and Orkneyinga saga also reference raven standards. The design — a raven on a cloth banner — is consistent across sources from different regions and periods.' },
  { q: 'What did the raven on the Viking banner mean?', a: 'The raven on the banner represented Odin\'s ravens Huginn (Thought) and Muninn (Memory) — symbols of the god\'s all-seeing wisdom and his role as the patron of warriors killed in battle (the einherjar who go to Valhöll). Carrying a raven banner was an invocation of Odin\'s favour. The saga tradition holds that if the banner\'s raven "flew" (the cloth rippled in the wind to suggest wing movement), victory was at hand; if it drooped, defeat was coming. This prophetic interpretation is recorded in the Orkneyinga saga account of Earl Sigurðr Hlöðvisson at the Battle of Clontarf (1014 CE).' },
];
