    <h1>The Viking Raven Banner: Hrafnsmerki in War and Mythology</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The raven banner (Old Norse: hrafnsmerki) was a real Viking Age war standard depicting a raven — the bird of Odin — used by Norse and Danish chieftains and kings. It is attested in multiple historical sources including the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, the Annals of St Neots (capturing a Danish raven banner at Ashdown, 871 CE), and the Orkneyinga saga account of the Battle of Clontarf (1014 CE). It was believed to predict victory if the raven "flew" (the cloth rippled in wind).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Historical Attestations</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The raven banner is documented in several independent contemporary and near-contemporary sources:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Annals of St Neots</strong> (c. 10th century CE): records that at the Battle of Ashdown (871 CE), King Æthelred and Alfred defeated the Danes and captured their standard — described as a raven banner said to have been woven by the daughters of Ragnar Loðbrók. The Danes were routed and the banner taken.</li>
      <li><strong>Anglo-Saxon Chronicle:</strong> multiple entries in the 9th–10th centuries mention raven standards among Danish forces operating in England.</li>
      <li><strong>Orkneyinga saga</strong> (c. 1230 CE, recording earlier events): Earl Sigurðr Hlöðvisson of Orkney carries the raven banner at the Battle of Clontarf (1014 CE). Three successive banner-carriers are killed; the Earl himself takes the banner and is killed. The saga states that victory follows the banner but death comes to the carrier.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Raven Banner and Odin</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The raven on the battle standard was explicitly connected to Odin's ravens Huginn and Muninn and to Odin's role as the god of battle and death:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Odin was the chooser of the slain — the einherjar killed in battle were taken to Valhöll. A raven standard invoked his attention and favour.</li>
      <li>Ravens were battlefield birds — they fed on the corpses of the slain. A raven banner in battle was both a symbol of the god and a promise of the bodies the ravens would feast on.</li>
      <li>The belief that the banner raven "flying" (cloth rippling) predicted victory reflects the bird's role as Odin's messenger: a raven flapping its wings was the god signalling his presence and approval.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The Old Norse kenning <em>hrafnáss</em> (Raven-god) refers to Odin, and raven banners served as a visual proclamation of the army's allegiance to the Allfather.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Banner of Ragnar Loðbrók's Daughters</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The Annals of St Neots state that the raven banner captured at Ashdown was woven in a single day by the daughters of Ragnar Loðbrók. The saga tradition adds that the banner had prophetic qualities: it would fly and predict victory when the Danes would triumph; it would droop when they would be defeated. This tradition links the banner to völva (seeress) craft and textile magic — raven imagery woven with prophetic intent.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

